Abstract
This paper introduces a deterministic, structure-driven behavioral generation model based on the 0–27 dimensional (0–27D) structural framework originally proposed by Wujie Gu (2026) in the World-Layer Model (WLM). The central hypothesis is that structural differentials (ΔD) generate tension gradients (ΔE), which project into the appearance layer (S) as measurable behavioral outputs (ΔS). Unlike traditional behavioral or language-generation systems that rely on semantic priors, emotional heuristics, or probabilistic patterning, WLM treats structure itself as the primary energetic substrate. Energy is not an external input but the dynamic expression of structural mismatch.
The paper is organized in two parts. Part One presents the theoretical foundations of the 0–27D system, defines the D/E/S tri-layer architecture, and proposes a fully measurable evaluation framework for structure-driven behavior generation. Part Two validates the model through controlled experiments using large language models (LLMs), demonstrating that structural inputs reliably modulate token consumption, reasoning depth, and behavioral coherence. A third major contribution — the Structure Alignment Protocol (SAP) — is formally integrated here as an operational implementation of the full model, providing readers with a concrete method for conditioning any LLM using the 0–27D structural system.
