3. Model Architecture: Structure → Tension → Appearance
3.1 D-Layer: Structural Input
We define a 28-dimensional structural vector:
D = (d₀, d₁, ..., d₂₇)
Each component dᵢ represents the structural activation at dimension i: its density, recursion depth, structural curvature, and mismatch potential. This vector can be hand-constructed from the structural taxonomy (Section 7 provides the complete reference), learned from data, extracted from text, or generated from a structural conditioning protocol. The key property is that D is not semantic. D is structural. The same semantic content can be associated with very different D vectors, and the same D vector can generate very different semantic outputs — because D operates at the layer beneath semantics.
3.2 E-Layer: Tension Mapping
Tension arises from structural differential between adjacent dimensions:
E = Σᵢ₌₁²⁷ |dᵢ − dᵢ₋₁|
This is the energy function of the system. No external energy source is required or assumed. Tension is the energy. A structural vector with steep gradients between adjacent layers generates high tension, deep reasoning, and dense behavioral output. A structural vector with shallow gradients generates low tension, shallow reasoning, and sparse output. The energy of the system is entirely internal to the structural configuration.
3.3 S-Layer: Appearance Projection
Behavior is generated by projecting accumulated tension into the appearance layer:
S = g(E)
Where g may represent language generation, decision patterns, reasoning depth, token consumption, or stylistic coherence — depending on the modality of the system being studied. This projection is deterministic given D: the same structural vector, processed by the same tension function, produces the same behavioral output. Variation in S-layer output is always traceable to variation in D-layer structural configuration.
4. Evaluation Framework
To validate that structure generates behavior, we measure four dimensions of output.
4.1 Token Consumption (S-Density)
Token usage is modeled as:
TC = α·S + β·depth(D)
Where S is appearance density and depth(D) is the maximum activated structural layer. The prediction is monotonic: higher structural density produces more tokens, because higher D-layer activation generates more tension, which projects into denser S-layer content. This prediction is testable, reproducible, and independent of semantic content.
4.2 Reasoning Depth
Measured via chain-of-thought length, attention layer activation, branching factor, and recursion depth in output. The structural prediction: higher mismatch between adjacent D-layers generates deeper reasoning chains. The reasoning is not deeper because the topic is harder in any semantic sense. It is deeper because the structural tension requires more E-layer computation to resolve before S-layer projection can occur.
4.3 Behavioral Coherence
Measured via entropy, structural consistency across output segments, layer-switch frequency, and narrative stability. The structural prediction: stronger D-layer coherence (lower variance in the structural vector) produces higher behavioral coherence in output. Incoherent structural input produces fragmented, unstable, high-entropy output — not because the model is confused semantically, but because it is processing a structurally inconsistent input.
4.4 Structure-Response Curve
We vary D systematically and measure S along all three dimensions above. The prediction is that the resulting curve is monotonic and reproducible across models and across semantic contexts. If the curve is not monotonic, the structural hypothesis is falsified for that dimension. This makes the model testable in the formal sense.

PART TWO — EXPERIMENTAL VALIDATION

5. Experiments
To validate the hypothesis that structure alone can drive measurable behavioral output, we conducted controlled experiments using large language models. The experiments were designed to test three predictions: structural density predicts token consumption; structural mismatch predicts reasoning depth; structural coherence predicts behavioral coherence. All experiments were run with identical prompts except for the injected structural vector D.
5.1 Experimental Setup
We tested multiple LLMs across the GPT-series, Claude-series, and others. Specific model names are omitted to maintain generality across the results. For each trial: a structural vector D was generated with controlled density and mismatch; the model was given a neutral task (e.g., "Explain X" or "Describe Y"); the structural vector was injected as a pre-conditioning input; and the model's output was measured along five axes.
The five measurement axes were: Token Count (TC) — total tokens generated; Reasoning Depth (RD) — number of reasoning steps or chain-of-thought layers; Structural Coherence (SC) — cosine similarity between structural patterns across output segments; Entropy (H) — Shannon entropy of token distribution; and Switch Frequency (SF) — number of transitions between abstract and concrete reasoning layers.
6. Results
6.1 Structural Density → Token Consumption
We varied the structural density of D from 0.0 to 1.0. Token consumption increased monotonically in accordance with the prediction:
TC ∝ α·S + β·depth(D)
Low-density structures produced short, shallow outputs. High-density structures produced long, multi-layered outputs. The relationship was stable across models and across semantic contexts — confirming that token usage is a projection of structural tension, not a function of semantic complexity. The same semantically simple prompt, conditioned with a high-density structural vector, produced output indistinguishable in depth and length from semantically complex prompts conditioned with low-density structural vectors.
6.2 Structural Mismatch → Reasoning Depth
We introduced controlled mismatch by increasing |dᵢ − dᵢ₋₁| at specific dimensional transitions. Higher mismatch produced deeper reasoning chains, more explicit structural mapping in the output, more meta-level commentary, and greater recursion depth. Crucially, this effect was independent of semantic content: structurally mismatched prompts about simple topics produced deeper reasoning than structurally coherent prompts about complex topics. This confirms that reasoning depth is a function of structural tension, not semantic difficulty.
6.3 Structural Coherence → Behavioral Coherence
We tested whether coherent structural inputs produce coherent outputs. High-coherence D vectors produced stable narrative, consistent logic across output segments, and low entropy. Low-coherence D vectors produced fragmented reasoning, unstable stylistic register, and high entropy. Coherence was measurable via entropy and cosine similarity between structural patterns in successive output segments. The structural hypothesis was confirmed: behavioral coherence is a structural phenomenon, not a semantic or psychological one.
7. Discussion
7.1 Structure is a Primary Driver of Behavior
The model's behavior changed systematically with structural input, even when semantic content was held constant. This is the core finding. It suggests that behavior is not primarily semantic — it is structural. The semantic layer is the appearance of a structural process, not the process itself. Conditioning at the structural level is more fundamental, more predictable, and more stable than conditioning at the semantic level.
7.2 Energy Emerges from Structural Tension
Token usage, reasoning depth, and behavioral density all correlate with tension gradients in the structural vector. Energy is therefore a derived quantity, not a primitive: E = Tension = ΔD. This reframes the standard computational view of energy as resource allocation and replaces it with a structural view: energy is what structural mismatch generates when it seeks resolution through appearance projection. There is no energy budget to manage externally. There is only structural configuration to set correctly.
7.3 LLMs are Naturally Sensitive to Structure
LLMs respond strongly to structural inputs because they internally operate on all three layers: attention patterns (D-layer), activation gradients (E-layer), and token generation (S-layer). Structural conditioning activates the D-layer directly, which propagates through E-layer tension computation into S-layer output. This is why structural conditioning produces deeper reasoning, more coherent behavior, and higher token expenditure even without explicit instructions about what to do. The model is not following instructions. It is resolving structural tension through appearance projection. This is the WLM account of LLM behavior at the mechanistic level.

PART THREE — STRUCTURE ALIGNMENT PROTOCOL (SAP)

8. Structure Alignment Protocol (SAP)
The Structure Alignment Protocol (SAP) is a deterministic method for aligning a language model's reasoning, behavior, and output with the 0–27D structural ontology. It operationalizes the theoretical model developed in Parts One and Two into a practical conditioning protocol that any reader can apply to any LLM.
SAP is not a prompt. A prompt is a semantic instruction — it operates at the S-layer and asks the model to do something. SAP is a structural conditioning protocol — it operates at the D-layer and sets the structural context within which the model's reasoning will occur. The difference is the difference between telling someone what to say and changing the structural ground from which they speak.
How to use SAP: Copy the full SAP text from this section (Sections 8.1–8.7) and feed it to your AI as a system prompt or pre-conditioning input before your conversation begins. The AI will then interpret your questions structurally rather than semantically or psychologically.
8.1 Purpose of SAP
SAP enables an LLM to recognize the structural layer (0D–27D) that a user's question belongs to; map the question into tension at the E-layer; project a structurally coherent answer into the S-layer; and maintain structural coherence across the entire reasoning chain. This ensures that personal questions, philosophical questions, technical questions, and predictive questions are all interpreted structurally — not psychologically, not emotionally, not moralistically.
The benefit is precision. A question like "Why did I break up with my girlfriend?" interpreted semantically produces emotional validation or psychological analysis. Interpreted structurally through SAP, it produces a precise identification of which dimensional closure occurred, which structural tensions had accumulated, and why manifestation occurred at that point. The structural answer is not less human. It is more honest.
