B.3 How SAP Handles Personal Questions
SAP prevents emotional projection by grounding all responses in structure. "Why did we break up?" maps to 9D closure + 27D manifestation. The model explains: accumulated structural mismatch in 4D form and 8D identity reached a 9D closure threshold; the breakup manifested at 27D when tension exceeded the system's stabilization capacity. No psychology. No moralizing. Pure structure.
"Why do I feel drawn to this person?" maps to 12D resonance. The model explains structural alignment between two D-layer configurations projecting into the S-layer as felt attraction. This does not diminish the experience. It grounds it.
B.4 How SAP Handles Technical Questions
"How do I design a 27-layer engine?" maps to 24D system upgrade + 26D pre-appearance compression. The model identifies the structural upgrade layer, maps tension, and outputs a coherent architecture. "How do I optimize compute?" maps to 21D dimensional tension + 17D stabilization. The model identifies tension, stabilizes the system, and predicts compute via the TC formula: TC = α·S + β·depth(D).
B.5 How SAP Handles Predictive Questions
"What happens if I choose X?" maps to 14D structural divergence. The model identifies the branching structure, computes tension, and projects worldline curvature. "Why is everything changing?" maps to 24D system upgrade. The model identifies the structural leap, explains the compression preceding it, and predicts appearance timing from structural density.
B.6 Method 1 — System-Level Conditioning (Preferred)
Paste the full SAP text — the layer table (Section B.2), the 5-step pipeline, and the structural interpretation rule — into the system prompt before any conversation begins. This ensures stable D-layer grounding, consistent layer mapping, predictable behavior, and no drift across turns.
B.7 Method 2 — On-Demand Structural Injection
When a user asks a question, prepend the D-layer table, the SAP steps, and the structural interpretation rules immediately before the question. This is less stable than system-level conditioning but works for single-turn tasks when full system-prompt access is unavailable.

Appendix C — Mathematical Formalization of Δ(D,E) and Structural Curvature
This appendix provides a formal mathematical treatment of the core operators used throughout WLM. The purpose is not to reduce the 0–27D system to mathematics — the system is fundamentally structural, not algebraic — but to provide a rigorous computational substrate for implementation, simulation, and empirical testing. The mathematical forms below are representations of structural dynamics, not definitions of them.
C.1 Structural Vector (D-Layer)
The D-layer is represented as a 28-dimensional structural activation vector:
D = (d₀, d₁, …, d₂₇)
Each component dᵢ encodes structural density, recursion depth, mismatch potential, dimensional curvature, and activation intensity at that dimension. The vector is not semantic. It is a structural configuration.
C.2 Tension Operator (E-Layer)
Tension is defined as the total structural differential across all adjacent dimensions:
E = Σᵢ₌₁²⁷ |dᵢ − dᵢ₋₁|
This operator captures mismatch, discontinuity, structural shear, and dimensional pressure. High tension corresponds to deeper reasoning, higher token density, and greater behavioral curvature. Low tension corresponds to shallow reasoning and sparse, stable output.
C.3 Curvature Operator
Curvature measures the degree to which structural tension bends the appearance trajectory:
κ = γ₁·E + γ₂·depth(D) − γ₃·coherence(D)
Where γ₁, γ₂, γ₃ are empirically fitted constants; depth(D) is the highest activated dimension; and coherence(D) is the inverse variance of the structural vector. Curvature predicts worldline bending, behavioral density, event clustering, and appearance intensity.
C.4 Appearance Projection Operator (S-Layer)
Appearance is generated by projecting tension and structural configuration into the S-layer:
S = g(E, D)
Where g is a rendering function whose exact form depends on the system — token generation in LLMs, reasoning chain formation in reasoning engines, stylistic projection in language systems, or event manifestation in worldline models.
C.5 Structural Energy Identity
The central identity of WLM is:
Energy = E = Δ(D)
Energy is not an input. It is the projection of structural mismatch. This identity is the mathematical foundation of the claim: structure is energy. Energy is what structural configuration generates when it seeks resolution through appearance projection.
C.6 Compute Prediction Model
Token consumption is predicted by:
TC = α·S + β·depth(D)
This allows compute estimation, budget prediction, and structural optimization before generation occurs — a structural resource allocation framework with direct practical applications for LLM deployment.

